\f1\fs22 At around ten, Tony was woken by buskers out in the street, squabbling over the better lyrics for a song. Sarah remained unconscious and face-planted in a pillow. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony rose to relieve himself. After experimenting with the various twinkling tones he could elicit from the toilet bowl by spraying this morning\'92s especially vigorous stream around, he thought of Sarah, and elected to wash his hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He never turned on the tap. Instead, a reflection in the mirror arrested his attention. He frowned and boggled, trying to make sense of what he saw. He closed and opened his eyes\'97together, then one at a time. He bared his teeth. He turned this way then that. He looked up his nostrils. He made monkey faces. All of these gestures the image in the mirror copied perfectly. It could not be true. Finally, he felt his face, and realised the mirror-mirror-on-the-wall knew who was the handsomest of them all. It was Tony.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He giggled with glee, and danced a little jig. He checked his forearms, then pulled his singlet off and examined his chest and back. The mirror confirmed all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He never felt so good. He lungs filled, pink with elasticity; his belly was a six-pack. No bones creaked, no teeth hurt. He felt several centimetres taller; and wondered if, perhaps, his receding hairline had un-receded somewhat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The best part? His burns had healed. Completely.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony felt invincible.\
