\f1\fs22 What was this miracle? Tony was within a fraction of an impulse of waking Sarah about it\'97he\'92d already lifted a pillow over shoulder height and was about to whack her\'97when she snored and snorted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony grimaced with distaste. \'91Not attractive,\'92 he uttered. All the mettle in his arms dissolved, and the pillow  felt heavier.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But she 
\f3\i was
\f1\i0  attractive, Tony thought, beautiful to the point of steeping him in a brew of longing that made his heart ache. He decided to let her sleep, but his renewed excitement, the unbounded puppy exuberance of his healing and renewal, demanded release. And so he marched, and the door opened, and the hallway seemed brighter and shorter than yesterday, and the stairwell less calamitously eroded with rotting steps, the Layer outside less acidic and somehow more invigorating, and the sun\'92s rays more potent as they warmed his renewed skin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His energy required he channel a purpose for it; a mission. And so\'97as if on the road to Damascus\'97he realised above all else that Sarah deserved a coffee.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A simple enough mission, the attainment of which became all the more elusive as Tony\'92s optimism sank. He should know better. In the Ghetto, it was never a question of whether you took half-and-half, sugar or amphetamines; it was a question of how much. Depending on the Elvi clone series, preferences could run as far as half a cup of half-and half, 418 cubes of sugar, and as much of Dr Nick\'92s Mix as you liked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony decided against loading Sarah with any of those extra stimulants, especially the half-and-half, so he continued to wander in search of a simple yoghurt frappuccino with raw sugar, caramel drizzle and blueberry topping.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was not to be, and as Tony became more intent of achieving his caffeinated aims, he wandered and zig-zagged until he quite lost his sense of direction, and wandered out of the Ghetto altogether.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s when four cat-sized hands of two wise guys grabbed him by the shoulders and knees, and bundled him into a shiny black suburban.\

